{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-05_15-41-13-hashicorp_raft-real-run && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('state.json'))\nfor c in d['question_candidates']:\n    if c['id']=='2324b095ff754f44aaf76f68b45df0a1':\n        print(json.dumps(c['question'], indent=1))\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"{\n \"audit_spec_version\": 1,\n \"disposition\": \"needs_specific_evidence\",\n \"preferred_check\": \"source_review\",\n \"question\": \"Is a heartbeat delivered through the transport fast path serialized with the main loop before it updates the receiver's term, state and believed leader, or can Raft.appendEntries run concurrently with runFollower/runCandidate on the same instance?\",\n \"importance\": \"The single-writer premise for the authority context (state, currentTerm, leaderAddr/leaderID) is what the whole election and step-down reasoning rests on. If an off-thread heartbeat can mutate that context while the main loop is in the middle of a campaign or a term change, a race could leave a node believing in a different leader/term than its own state machine; the consequence is a wrong authority view rather than a memory-safety crash, so the impact is bounded but the premise matters.\",\n \"source_ids\": [\n  \"SR-LEASE\",\n  \"SR-RUN-FOLLOWER\",\n  \"SR-APPEND-ENTRIES\",\n  \"SR-RPC-HB\",\n  \"SR-NET-HB\",\n  \"SR-HB-HANDLER\"\n ],\n \"participants\": [],\n \"objects\": [],\n \"contexts\": [\n  \"follower receiving leader heartbeats\",\n  \"candidate campaigning while a heartbeat from a known leader arrives\"\n ],\n \"event_paths\": [\n  \"NetworkTransport.handleConn classifies a request as a pure heartbeat and calls heartbeatFn directly\",\n  \"Raft.processHeartbeat dispatches to Raft.appendEntries\",\n  \"a concurrent main-loop iteration of runFollower or runCandidate reads and writes the same authority context\"\n ],\n \"activity_classes\": [\n  \"A2\"\n ],\n \"behavior_ids\": [\n  \"b-heartbeat-timeout\"\n ],\n \"fact_ids\": [\n  \"f-leadership-validity\"\n ],\n \"supporting_behavior_ids\": {\n  \"b-follower-append\": \"the off-thread path that actually applies the heartbeat's term/leader update\"\n },\n \"obligation_relation_kind\": \"consumption\",\n \"counterevidence\": [\n  \"setState and setCurrentTerm use atomic stores and setLeader uses a mutex, so a pure heartbeat does not obviously produce a torn read of any single field\",\n  \"a pure heartbeat carries no entries, no previous-entry index and a zero leader commit index, so it skips the log, configuration and commit branches of appendEntries\"\n ],\n \"unknowns\": [\n  \"Which fields a pure heartbeat actually mutates on the receiver, and whether the existing atomic/locked access already provides the needed ordering\",\n  \"Whether the main loop can observe the update while it is inside runCandidate's vote tally rather than only at a select boundary\"\n ],\n \"priority\": 1,\n \"trigger_rationale\": \"Raft.appendEntries is documented as callable only from the main thread, yet NewRaft installs Raft.processHeartbeat (which calls appendEntries) as the transport heartbeat handler, and NetworkTransport.handleConn, started as a goroutine per connection, invokes that handler directly for pure heartbeats. That is a concrete, sourced difference in the authority-context path; bounding which fields a pure heartbeat actually mutates and whether the existing atomic/locked access already serializes them decides whether the comment is stale or the code unsafe.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
