{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"submission\": \"submission.json\",\n  \"summary\": \"Submitted an 'explained' resolution for the paused high-consequence heartbeat-fast-path candidate.\\n\\nContext from the index: both findings remain confirmed_in_scope, map v4 is the accepted basis, pending_work is empty, and capacity still holds 2 audit units, 4 semantic reviews and 6 experiments. The open frontier included b-follower-append's heartbeat premise, which the map marks as a high-consequence surface.\\n\\nProduct contents (draft-relative submission.json): action=explained on candidate 2324b095ff754f44aaf76f68b45df0a1, with the question text, contexts, event paths, activity/behavior/fact basis, obligation relation and supporting-behavior wording kept identical to the saved basis so the refinement stays on its accepted map version rather than reinterpreting a historical basis.\\n\\nThe protective mechanism cited: NetworkTransport classifies a request as a fast-path heartbeat only when it carries a nonzero term, no previous log entry, no entries and a zero leader commit index, so appendEntries takes no log, configuration or commit branch; the fields such a request does touch are individually serialized (state and currentTerm as atomic stores/loads, leaderAddr/leaderID under leaderLock, lastContact under lastContactLock, observer delivery under observersLock). The documented main-thread-only rule is therefore stronger than the implementation's actual dependency.\\n\\nThe submission keeps two named residual unknowns instead of claiming more: the (setState, setCurrentTerm) pair is not atomic as a pair, so an off-thread heartbeat and a concurrent campaign can still interleave at the field level and a term regression has not been excluded; and the shutdown branch of processHeartbeat returns without answering the RPC, which a transport consumer waiting for a response would observe. The accompanying feedback records the answered proposition and both remaining items.\\n\\nPreflight `validate` returned valid=true with no diagnostics. Acceptance and the resulting candidate status remain with the controller.\"\n}"}}
